WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.056
This is the executive
briefing for Book 06 of the

3
00:00:04.056 --> 00:00:07.762
Federal Modernization
Workbook, SQL Server identity.

4
00:00:07.762 --> 00:00:11.416
It's for the people who make
the calls: the CIO, the CISO,

5
00:00:11.416 --> 00:00:15.401
the data platform owner,
application owners and sponsors.

6
00:00:15.401 --> 00:00:16.825
The argument is short.

7
00:00:16.825 --> 00:00:21.042
Every domain-joined SQL Server
inherited Windows Authentication,

8
00:00:21.042 --> 00:00:26.087
and none of it is visible to the identity
plane that enforces multifactor sign-in.

9
00:00:26.087 --> 00:00:30.175
The estate has to be counted and
shrunk before any login moves.

10
00:00:30.175 --> 00:00:34.297
And the destination is an Entra
token on every connection,

11
00:00:34.297 --> 00:00:38.162
finished well before the
program's own NTLM block date.

12
00:00:38.162 --> 00:00:42.538
Each decision slide names the decision,
its owner and when it's needed.

13
00:00:42.538 --> 00:00:45.900
Let's start with why the
database is where this hides.

14
00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:49.925
Why does legacy authentication
hide in the database?

15
00:00:49.925 --> 00:00:54.125
Because SQL Server doesn't check
a Windows user's password itself.

16
00:00:54.125 --> 00:00:57.813
It trusts whatever Windows tells
it, and you can't switch that off.

17
00:00:57.813 --> 00:01:01.247
Which protocol a connection
actually used is visible

18
00:01:01.247 --> 00:01:04.300
only from inside the engine,
one session at a time.

19
00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:08.892
The dependencies sit in connection strings,
service accounts and linked servers,

20
00:01:08.892 --> 00:01:11.525
and none of them reaches
an identity dashboard.

21
00:01:11.525 --> 00:01:14.847
Of the four ways a connection
can authenticate today,

22
00:01:14.847 --> 00:01:19.000
only an Entra token passes through
multifactor and device checks.

23
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.751
So when NTLM goes and
Conditional Access arrives,

24
00:01:22.751 --> 00:01:25.588
the database breaks last and hardest.

25
00:01:25.588 --> 00:01:26.974
It needs its own plan.

26
00:01:26.974 --> 00:01:28.950
And the worst of it is silent.

27
00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:31.475
Here's the part nobody sees.

28
00:01:31.475 --> 00:01:35.053
When an instance's service
principal name is missing,

29
00:01:35.053 --> 00:01:39.850
Kerberos quietly gives way to NTLM,
and the application still connects.

30
00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:44.551
The engine doesn't log which protocol
each session used, so an estate that

31
00:01:44.551 --> 00:01:49.800
believes it runs Kerberos can be running
NTLM on a large share of its connections.

32
00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:51.338
Only measurement tells you.

33
00:01:51.338 --> 00:01:55.658
Meanwhile the identities themselves
are anchored in Active Directory,

34
00:01:55.658 --> 00:02:00.051
or in a password the instance checks on
its own, like the built-in sa account.

35
00:02:00.051 --> 00:02:01.625
None of this was a mistake.

36
00:02:01.625 --> 00:02:05.700
It was good engineering for a world
where everything was domain-joined.

37
00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:09.500
That world is ending, and
three drivers are ending it.

38
00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:12.612
Three forces end this inheritance.

39
00:02:12.612 --> 00:02:15.652
NTLM is going: Microsoft
has deprecated it,

40
00:02:15.652 --> 00:02:19.126
and the program has
committed to eliminating it.

41
00:02:19.126 --> 00:02:24.625
Zero trust needs the Entra identity
plane in front of every human sign-in.

42
00:02:24.625 --> 00:02:27.518
And when Active Directory
stops being the source,

43
00:02:27.518 --> 00:02:30.200
the engine needs an
identity that outlives it.

44
00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:34.699
April first, 2027 is the
program's own NTLM block date,

45
00:02:34.699 --> 00:02:37.838
not a Microsoft or federal deadline.

46
00:02:37.838 --> 00:02:43.123
The outside dates in 2027 are support dates
for older Windows Server and SQL Server,

47
00:02:43.123 --> 00:02:46.187
and none of those versions
can take Entra logins.

48
00:02:46.187 --> 00:02:50.791
So the decision is to hold the
order: count and consolidate,

49
00:02:50.791 --> 00:02:53.860
enable Arc, move logins, then block NTLM.

50
00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:55.960
Counting comes first.

51
00:02:56.710 --> 00:02:59.210
Before anyone decides anything, count.

52
00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:04.311
Twenty years of growth leave three kinds
of sprawl: more instances than anyone

53
00:03:04.311 --> 00:03:09.648
remembers, databases nobody can account
for, and dependencies that cross domains.

54
00:03:09.648 --> 00:03:14.118
No single tool finds them all, so
the program combines a network sweep,

55
00:03:14.118 --> 00:03:17.333
the Kerberos registration
trail and Microsoft's

56
00:03:17.333 --> 00:03:20.548
assessment tools into one
inventory it can re-run.

57
00:03:20.548 --> 00:03:22.835
The book's illustration makes the point.

58
00:03:22.835 --> 00:03:25.587
A team that believes it
has forty instances,

59
00:03:25.587 --> 00:03:29.410
when discovery finds a hundred
and ten, isn't behind schedule.

60
00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:32.622
Its whole estimate is wrong
by nearly three times.

61
00:03:32.622 --> 00:03:37.122
Missing instances surface at the worst
possible moment unless you look first.

62
00:03:37.122 --> 00:03:39.340
Then each one needs a verdict.

63
00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:43.559
Every instance gets one of
three verdicts: retain it,

64
00:03:43.559 --> 00:03:47.028
consolidate its workload
into a target, or retire it.

65
00:03:47.028 --> 00:03:49.377
Three lenses make the call defensible.

66
00:03:49.377 --> 00:03:50.590
How busy is it?

67
00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:53.778
A database untouched for
over a year is a zombie,

68
00:03:53.778 --> 00:03:57.702
and retiring it removes every
forgotten login it carries.

69
00:03:57.702 --> 00:03:58.878
What version is it?

70
00:03:58.878 --> 00:04:01.960
Entra logins need SQL
Server 2022 or later,

71
00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:06.277
so most old engines are better
consolidated than upgraded.

72
00:04:06.277 --> 00:04:07.578
And what depends on it?

73
00:04:07.578 --> 00:04:11.653
A job that runs once a quarter can
make an idle instance load-bearing.

74
00:04:11.653 --> 00:04:16.715
The decision for the CIO and data
leadership: a verdict for every instance

75
00:04:16.715 --> 00:04:21.528
and a named target, so authentication
migrates once, in its final shape.

76
00:04:21.528 --> 00:04:22.890
Then the audit.

77
00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:27.269
The audit asks one question
of every surviving instance:

78
00:04:27.269 --> 00:04:29.740
what is actually authenticating here?

79
00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:35.640
It records the logins, the mode, the service
account and the Kerberos registration,

80
00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:38.590
and it flags the worst
first: an enabled sa,

81
00:04:38.590 --> 00:04:41.627
active SQL logins, missing registrations.

82
00:04:41.627 --> 00:04:44.690
Each instance then lands in
one of three categories.

83
00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:45.990
Ready for Entra now.

84
00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:48.140
Ready once Azure Arc is installed.

85
00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:53.390
Or blocked, because the version is older
than 2022 or it's a failover cluster.

86
00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:58.185
The decision: data leadership upgrades
or retires each blocked instance it's

87
00:04:58.185 --> 00:05:01.181
keeping, and anything that
can't move in time goes

88
00:05:01.181 --> 00:05:04.253
to the authorizing official
as a dated exception.

89
00:05:04.253 --> 00:05:06.010
Here's the destination.

90
00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:08.673
The destination is simple to say.

91
00:05:08.673 --> 00:05:11.009
Every connection carries an Entra token.

92
00:05:11.009 --> 00:05:14.773
Azure Arc gives each instance
an identity in Entra.

93
00:05:14.773 --> 00:05:18.145
People, groups and
applications get Entra logins,

94
00:05:18.145 --> 00:05:20.860
and sa is disabled rather than migrated.

95
00:05:20.860 --> 00:05:26.036
Multifactor is enforced by Conditional
Access before a token is ever issued;

96
00:05:26.036 --> 00:05:28.948
the engine itself has
no multifactor logic.

97
00:05:28.948 --> 00:05:30.535
One point saves months.

98
00:05:30.535 --> 00:05:34.335
The engine can move to Entra while
the server is still domain-joined.

99
00:05:34.335 --> 00:05:36.873
Waiting for Windows sign-in to move first

100
00:05:36.873 --> 00:05:39.485
is the most common
cause of needless delay.

101
00:05:39.485 --> 00:05:43.040
So the decision for server
infrastructure: onboard Arc now,

102
00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:45.797
starting with the instances
that need it most.

103
00:05:45.797 --> 00:05:48.150
Five positions govern that work.

104
00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:54.450
The engine-authentication decision, still
formally proposed, settles five positions.

105
00:05:54.450 --> 00:05:56.400
Consolidation comes first.

106
00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:01.045
Every production cutover runs in
parallel: create the Entra login,

107
00:06:01.045 --> 00:06:05.775
prove it works, disable the old one,
watch for thirty days, then drop it.

108
00:06:05.775 --> 00:06:09.928
SQL logins are eliminated, and
sa is disabled and renamed,

109
00:06:09.928 --> 00:06:13.463
with an empty break-glass
group for emergencies.

110
00:06:13.463 --> 00:06:16.775
Older versions upgrade or
carry a dated exception.

111
00:06:16.775 --> 00:06:19.663
And a per-instance record
is the proof of closure.

112
00:06:19.663 --> 00:06:23.512
The decision belongs to the
CISO: set the standard once,

113
00:06:23.512 --> 00:06:28.449
SQL authentication refused and sa
disabled everywhere in production.

114
00:06:28.449 --> 00:06:32.422
An instance that can't meet it
doesn't stay in mixed mode quietly;

115
00:06:32.422 --> 00:06:34.713
it goes to the authorizing official.

116
00:06:34.713 --> 00:06:36.960
Now, where all this runs.

117
00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:39.835
Two facts shape where this runs.

118
00:06:39.835 --> 00:06:44.806
Entra authentication for Arc-connected SQL
Server isn't offered in the US Government

119
00:06:44.806 --> 00:06:49.496
Virginia region today, so the design uses
a commercial Azure subscription in the

120
00:06:49.496 --> 00:06:51.877
same directory as the government tenant,

121
00:06:51.877 --> 00:06:54.397
and the hosts need a path
to the public cloud.

122
00:06:54.397 --> 00:06:58.553
The services it leans on are each
listed in scope for FedRAMP High,

123
00:06:58.553 --> 00:07:00.861
but a listing isn't an authorization.

124
00:07:00.861 --> 00:07:05.797
On cost, Entra authentication itself
works under a plain SQL Server license;

125
00:07:05.797 --> 00:07:11.360
the extras are Conditional Access, privileged
access management and Defender plans.

126
00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:15.839
So the authorizing official decides:
accept these leveraged services,

127
00:07:15.839 --> 00:07:19.710
after reviewing each package,
before production onboarding.

128
00:07:19.710 --> 00:07:21.620
Now, who decides what.

129
00:07:22.370 --> 00:07:28.020
Under Windows authentication, the DBA team
owned database access almost by default.

130
00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:32.461
In the target, one connection depends
on a group the identity team built,

131
00:07:32.461 --> 00:07:37.203
a rule the data owner approved, a permission
only a privileged role can grant,

132
00:07:37.203 --> 00:07:42.095
a login the DBAs created, and a connection
string the application team changed.

133
00:07:42.095 --> 00:07:45.019
Miss any owner and the
gap shows up as drift,

134
00:07:45.019 --> 00:07:47.608
or as a help desk call nobody can close.

135
00:07:47.608 --> 00:07:49.920
So the model is one decider per row.

136
00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:52.850
Data leadership decides
verdicts and closure;

137
00:07:52.850 --> 00:07:57.808
the authorizing official decides every
exception; the CISO sets the standard.

138
00:07:57.808 --> 00:08:01.919
The first step is filling the
project's open sponsor and lead roles.

139
00:08:01.919 --> 00:08:04.140
So what does each team actually do?

140
00:08:04.890 --> 00:08:06.128
So who does the work?

141
00:08:06.128 --> 00:08:10.530
The DBAs run the engine: the
inventory, the audit, the logins,

142
00:08:10.530 --> 00:08:14.566
the thirty-day parallel run,
and the Kerberos repairs.

143
00:08:14.566 --> 00:08:16.828
What they no longer decide is who gets in.

144
00:08:16.828 --> 00:08:21.890
Membership of the group that holds sysadmin
becomes a privileged access decision.

145
00:08:21.890 --> 00:08:24.885
Application owners own
their side of the chain:

146
00:08:24.885 --> 00:08:28.340
connection strings, test
sign-off and cutover windows.

147
00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:32.817
And data owners, even when they're the
same person, answer a different question:

148
00:08:32.817 --> 00:08:35.965
does this access rule still
describe the right people?

149
00:08:35.965 --> 00:08:38.928
The decision for
application and data owners:

150
00:08:38.928 --> 00:08:42.202
confirm every rule before
its group grants access.

151
00:08:42.202 --> 00:08:45.820
Some steps sit outside
the DBA team on purpose.

152
00:08:46.570 --> 00:08:51.083
Some of the most powerful steps don't
belong to DBAs at all, by design.

153
00:08:51.083 --> 00:08:55.902
Granting the instance identity its
directory permissions takes a role that can

154
00:08:55.902 --> 00:08:58.132
manage every other role in the tenant,

155
00:08:58.132 --> 00:09:01.945
so it stays with a small identity
group, eligible only on request.

156
00:09:01.945 --> 00:09:06.045
And the Azure roles around the server
now matter as much as sysadmin.

157
00:09:06.045 --> 00:09:09.507
Contributor on the server can
run privileged extensions.

158
00:09:09.507 --> 00:09:13.721
Contributor on the SQL resource
can switch SQL logins back on.

159
00:09:13.721 --> 00:09:16.832
Whoever holds those roles
holds a database privilege.

160
00:09:16.832 --> 00:09:20.327
So the CISO's decision: review
those Azure assignments on

161
00:09:20.327 --> 00:09:23.894
the same cadence as sysadmin,
from the first Arc onboarding.

162
00:09:23.894 --> 00:09:26.590
Next, the applications upstream.

163
00:09:27.340 --> 00:09:31.302
The database isn't finished until
the applications in front of it are.

164
00:09:31.302 --> 00:09:35.287
Each application gets placed on
two axes: how its users sign in,

165
00:09:35.287 --> 00:09:38.178
and what its database
driver can actually do,

166
00:09:38.178 --> 00:09:41.928
from a simple configuration
change to no Entra path at all.

167
00:09:41.928 --> 00:09:46.677
An application stuck on legacy domain
services holds its whole instance back.

168
00:09:46.677 --> 00:09:51.570
The change itself is one line in a connection
string, but it waits for three gates:

169
00:09:51.570 --> 00:09:53.794
the login exists, the role is granted,

170
00:09:53.794 --> 00:09:57.352
and it's been tested somewhere
that looks like production.

171
00:09:57.352 --> 00:10:01.502
Application owners approve each
cutover against those gates.

172
00:10:01.502 --> 00:10:03.810
Then, the protocol itself.

173
00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:07.348
NTLM isn't a setting you switch off.

174
00:10:07.348 --> 00:10:11.058
It's a protocol that four
connection patterns keep producing,

175
00:10:11.058 --> 00:10:13.022
and each has to be found and fixed.

176
00:10:13.022 --> 00:10:15.435
The program does it in three phases.

177
00:10:15.435 --> 00:10:17.223
Discover every source.

178
00:10:17.223 --> 00:10:20.056
Restrict: block the oldest version first,

179
00:10:20.056 --> 00:10:24.973
repair Kerberos registrations, move
linked servers and applications.

180
00:10:24.973 --> 00:10:30.272
Then eliminate, with a domain-wide
block on April first, 2027.

181
00:10:30.272 --> 00:10:33.972
Any instance already on
Entra logins doesn't notice.

182
00:10:33.972 --> 00:10:38.235
The block lives in Windows, so it
ignores engine-level exceptions.

183
00:10:38.235 --> 00:10:41.448
That leaves one open question
for the NTLM program,

184
00:10:41.448 --> 00:10:44.966
with each exception going to
the authorizing official:

185
00:10:44.966 --> 00:10:49.785
does the date hold for the long tail, or
does it get dated, isolated exceptions?

186
00:10:49.785 --> 00:10:52.380
Certificates are the next dependency.

187
00:10:53.130 --> 00:10:58.305
SQL Server leans on certificates in three
places most retirement plans can't see.

188
00:10:58.305 --> 00:11:01.912
The TLS certificate every
Entra connection now requires,

189
00:11:01.912 --> 00:11:04.842
since those connections
are always encrypted.

190
00:11:04.842 --> 00:11:07.967
Certificates behind signed
code and endpoints.

191
00:11:07.967 --> 00:11:12.793
And certificate sign-in, the stopgap for
connections that can't reach Entra in time,

192
00:11:12.793 --> 00:11:16.767
which quietly depends on the domain
controllers' own certificates.

193
00:11:16.767 --> 00:11:20.693
Cloud PKI can't help here;
it doesn't issue to servers.

194
00:11:20.693 --> 00:11:25.945
Book 07 settles the issuer: a retained
internal hierarchy, renewed through the

195
00:11:25.945 --> 00:11:28.935
lifecycle platform, with domain controller

196
00:11:28.935 --> 00:11:32.005
certificates kept until
the last one retires.

197
00:11:32.005 --> 00:11:36.088
The decision for data leadership:
set no retirement date for the old

198
00:11:36.088 --> 00:11:40.243
certificate authority until every
SQL Server dependency has moved.

199
00:11:40.243 --> 00:11:42.050
Then, what done means.

200
00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:43.887
Done isn't a date.

201
00:11:43.887 --> 00:11:45.612
It's a posture you keep proving.

202
00:11:45.612 --> 00:11:50.756
An instance is closed when five things
hold at once: no usable Windows logins,

203
00:11:50.756 --> 00:11:54.921
SQL authentication refused,
Entra sign-ins observed working,

204
00:11:54.921 --> 00:11:59.412
an Entra identity for the engine,
and multifactor for every person.

205
00:11:59.412 --> 00:12:04.775
Live evidence shows it over a validation
period, and any drift is a violation.

206
00:12:04.775 --> 00:12:10.087
That's why April first, 2027 is
a consequence, not a finish line.

207
00:12:10.087 --> 00:12:14.000
Migration ends well before it, and
the last months are for proving.

208
00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:18.486
The decision for data leadership, as
system owner: declare closure only

209
00:12:18.486 --> 00:12:23.200
on the security officer's evidence,
with every exception already accepted.

210
00:12:23.200 --> 00:12:24.910
What could still go wrong?

211
00:12:25.660 --> 00:12:28.698
Here are the risks the book
names, in three groups.

212
00:12:28.698 --> 00:12:33.499
Scope: instances found late, cluster
instances that can't take Entra logins,

213
00:12:33.499 --> 00:12:36.597
and applications that
hold their instance back.

214
00:12:36.597 --> 00:12:40.031
Sequence: engine work held up
waiting for Windows sign-in,

215
00:12:40.031 --> 00:12:43.903
the old certificate authority
retired before its replacement,

216
00:12:43.903 --> 00:12:46.898
and migration still running
when the block lands.

217
00:12:46.898 --> 00:12:51.188
Control: Azure roles that can
quietly turn SQL logins back on,

218
00:12:51.188 --> 00:12:55.736
and one server identity shared by
every application on the host.

219
00:12:55.736 --> 00:13:00.188
The decision: every residual risk
goes in the exception registry,

220
00:13:00.188 --> 00:13:05.547
with a control, an owner and an end date,
and the authorizing official accepts it.

221
00:13:05.547 --> 00:13:07.140
One last question.

222
00:13:07.890 --> 00:13:11.630
Once authentication has moved,
a different question opens up:

223
00:13:11.630 --> 00:13:14.490
which platform should
each data domain live on?

224
00:13:14.490 --> 00:13:18.678
That choice isn't forced, so it
comes after, one domain at a time.

225
00:13:18.678 --> 00:13:22.397
Stay on SQL Server, or move
to PostgreSQL or Oracle;

226
00:13:22.397 --> 00:13:25.391
each can meet the identity requirement.

227
00:13:25.391 --> 00:13:30.269
The book scores them on ten dimensions
with evidence, from migration effort and

228
00:13:30.269 --> 00:13:32.339
skills to licensing and exit cost,

229
00:13:32.339 --> 00:13:35.665
and two domains reaching
different answers is normal.

230
00:13:35.665 --> 00:13:39.428
The goal is fewer authoritative
platforms, not one vendor.

231
00:13:39.428 --> 00:13:43.922
So the decision for the CIO and
data leadership: choose per domain,

232
00:13:43.922 --> 00:13:49.415
after closure, as its own funded decision,
with the same parallel-run discipline.

233
00:13:49.415 --> 00:13:51.630
Let's pull the decisions together.

234
00:13:52.380 --> 00:13:53.955
So, the decisions.

235
00:13:53.955 --> 00:13:56.703
The CIO and data
leadership hold the order,

236
00:13:56.703 --> 00:14:01.414
give every instance a verdict and a
target, upgrade or retire old engines,

237
00:14:01.414 --> 00:14:05.811
set no retirement date for the old
certificate authority until SQL

238
00:14:05.811 --> 00:14:10.130
Server's certificates have moved,
and declare closure on evidence.

239
00:14:10.130 --> 00:14:14.180
The CISO sets the standard and
reviews Azure roles like sysadmin.

240
00:14:14.180 --> 00:14:18.605
Server infrastructure onboards
Arc now, on domain-joined hosts.

241
00:14:18.605 --> 00:14:23.155
Application and data owners approve
their access rules and their cutovers.

242
00:14:23.155 --> 00:14:28.030
The authorizing official accepts the
leveraged services and every exception.

243
00:14:28.030 --> 00:14:31.380
And the NTLM program
decides the long tail.

244
00:14:31.380 --> 00:14:33.243
None of this waits on a purchase.

245
00:14:33.243 --> 00:14:34.906
Count the estate first.

246
00:14:34.906 --> 00:14:38.967
Book 06 has the detail,
every claim footnoted.

247
00:14:38.967 --> 00:14:39.780
Thank you.
